{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>How did you make a breach for yourself? — Peretz.</b> You forced yourself with force — this is the language of Rashi. The meaning of פרץ everywhere is the breaking down of a fence and passing through it, as in פרוץ גדרו (Isaiah 5:5), למה פרצת גדריה (Psalms 80:13), and in their language (Sukkah 26a) they call a thief a פרצה. But in the sacred tongue it is said of any crossing of a boundary: ופרצת ימה וקדמה (Genesis 28:14), ויפרוץ האיש מאד מאד (Genesis 30:43). Therefore it says here, concerning the first one — when he drew back his hand and he hastened to come out — מה פרצת, how you breached a great breach in the fence, to hurry out of it. And it says עליך because the fence was upon him and he was confined within it, that is to say, how you breached upon yourself a great breach in the fence, and went out of it.\n\nRabbi Avraham said: מה פרצת is like a man who breaches a fence and goes out of it, and behold, this breach is upon you as a judgment. But there is no sense in this.\n\nIn the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah, a secret is mentioned in connection with the names of these newborns. They said: he is called זרח on account of the sun, which shines constantly; and פרץ on account of the moon, which is breached at times and built up at times. Now Peretz is the firstborn, and the sun is greater than the moon. There is no difficulty, for it is written ויתן יד, and it is written ואחר יצא אחיו. According to their view, the name of the moon was given to Peretz because of the kingdom of the House of David, and they were twins because the moon is matched to the sun; and behold, Peretz is the twin of Zerach, who gave the hand, yet he is the firstborn in supernal potency, as it says (Psalms 89:28), אף אני בכור אתנהו. This is the meaning of their statement (Rosh HaShanah 25a) in the sanctification of the new month: David king of Israel lives and endures. And the enlightened will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes the Bahir (sec. 196) in the name of R. Nechunya b. HaKanah; the printed Hebrew reads ספר הבהיר קצו (i.e., section 196). The Aramaic phrases לא קשיא and אקרי are rendered literally without smoothing. The cryptic final line והמשכיל יבין is left untranslated per the rail on kabbalistic passages."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:19.270140443Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}